### Files in C – A Quick Primer

In C, a **file** is simply a persistent sequence of bytes stored outside the program’s memory (on disk, flash, network, etc.).  
C gives you a thin abstraction over the underlying OS I/O facilities through the **stdio** library (`<stdio.h>`). The key concepts are:

| Concept | What it means |
|---------|---------------|
| `FILE *` | An opaque pointer that represents an open file stream. You never see its internals; you just pass it around. |
| `fopen()` / `freopen()` | Open a file and get a `FILE *`. |
| `fclose()` | Close a file stream. |
| `fread()` / `fwrite()` | Read/write raw bytes (binary mode). |
| `fprintf()` / `fscanf()` | Write/read formatted text (text mode). |
| `fgets()` / `fputs()` | Read/write lines of text. |
| Modes (`"r"`, `"w"`, `"a"`, `"rb"`, `"wb+"`, …) | Tell the OS how you intend to use the file (read, write, append, binary/text, update). |

---

#### 1. Opening a File

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    /* Open for reading text */
    FILE *fp = fopen("example.txt", "r");
    if (!fp) { perror("fopen"); return 1; }

    /* ... use fp ... */

    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
```

*If you’re on Windows and want to avoid CRLF translation, use `"rb"` or `"wb"`.*

---

#### 2. Reading & Writing

##### Text Mode (formatted)

```c
char line[256];
while (fgets(line, sizeof line, fp)) {
    printf("%s", line);          // prints each line
}
```

##### Binary Mode (raw bytes)

```c
unsigned char buffer[128];
size_t n = fread(buffer, 1, sizeof buffer, fp);
if (n == 0 && ferror(fp)) perror("fread");
```

---

#### 3. Updating (Read + Write)

```c
FILE *fp = fopen("data.bin", "r+b");   // read/write binary
/* Seek to position 10 */
fseek(fp, 10, SEEK_SET);
/* Write 4 bytes */
fwrite(&value, sizeof value, 1, fp);
fclose(fp);
```

---

#### 4. Error Handling

| Function | Typical error indicator | How to check |
|----------|------------------------|--------------|
| `fopen()` | NULL | `if (!fp)` |
| `fread()/fwrite()` | Short count | Compare returned count to requested count |
| `fseek()` | -1 | `if (fseek(...) != 0)` |
| `feof()` | End‑of‑file reached | `if (feof(fp))` |
| `ferror()` | I/O error | `if (ferror(fp))` |

Use `perror()` or `strerror(errno)` for human‑readable messages.

---

#### 5. Common Pitfalls

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| Newline conversion on Windows | `"r"`/`"w"` translate `\r\n` ↔ `\n` | Use `"rb"`/`"wb"` for binary or `"r"`/`"w"` only when you want text semantics. |
| Forgetting `fflush()` before `fseek()` | Data may still be buffered | Call `fflush(fp)` or close/reopen. |
| Mixing `printf()` and `fwrite()` on same stream | Buffering conflicts | Flush after `printf()` or use `setvbuf()` to set unbuffered mode. |
| Not checking return values | Silent failures | Always test return codes. |

---

#### 6. Example: Copying a File

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int copy_file(const char *src, const char *dst) {
    FILE *in  = fopen(src, "rb");
    FILE *out = fopen(dst, "wb");
    if (!in || !out) { perror("fopen"); return 1; }

    unsigned char buf[4096];
    size_t n;
    while ((n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, in)) > 0) {
        if (fwrite(buf, 1, n, out) != n) { perror("fwrite"); return 1; }
    }
    if (ferror(in)) { perror("fread"); return 1; }

    fclose(in);
    fclose(out);
    return 0;
}

int main(void) {
    return copy_file("source.dat", "dest.dat");
}
```

---

#### 7. Where to Learn More

- **ISO C Standard** – Sections 7.21.1–7.21.9 cover stdio.
- **K&R “The C Programming Language”** – Chapter 11 (“Input/Output”) explains streams.
- **POSIX `man 3 fopen`** – On Unix-like systems, shows platform specifics.
- **Microsoft Docs** – `fopen`, `fread`, etc. for Windows quirks.

---

### TL;DR

- A **file** in C is represented by a `FILE *`.
- Use `fopen()`/`fclose()` to manage lifetime.
- Choose the right mode (`"r"`, `"w"`, `"rb"`, `"wb+"`, …).
- Handle errors explicitly; don’t ignore return values.
- Remember newline translation on Windows unless you open in binary mode.

Happy coding!
